          <circle class="lp-fig-good" cx="406" cy="272" r="5"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="418" y="276">Meets the need</text>

        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">5 of 5 needs on. Options that fit: LightPhotos.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Each option failed one different need. Turn a need off to see
          which options then fit.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your photos as plain files.</strong> A backup helps only
          if an app can open it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buying software once does not mean that you can always use it.</strong>
          A macOS upgrade stopped my Lightroom license, and nothing warned me.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More features made the free editors harder for me.</strong>
          They could do everything, so I could not get started.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the list before the code.</strong> The list helped me
          say no to everything else.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Nothing fit</h2>
      <p>
        I am an amateur photographer. I shoot RAW with my Sony full-frame
        camera, so the camera saves the unprocessed sensor data, and most
        shots need a little work before I share them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Luminar AI is good, but its experience and performance are not what I
        wanted. A Lightroom subscription costs $10 each month for an app that
        I open only sometimes.
      </p>
      <p>
        I like Apple Photos. It is beautiful and fast. But it keeps the full
        library in a single file, in a format that only Apple Photos can read.
        A newer version of macOS did not open the Photos libraries on my older
        backups, and several years of pictures went with them. I do not
        control that format, and I will not take that risk again.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 208" role="img" aria-label="Two paths for the same photos. In a locked library, your photos become one library file, and they are lost after an upgrade. As ordinary files, your photos stay ordinary files, and any app can open them.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A BACKUP THAT YOU CANNOT OPEN IS NOT A BACKUP</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">Locked in a library</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="73" x2="214" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 68 L224 73 L214 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="78" text-anchor="middle">One library file</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="73" x2="422" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 68 L432 73 L422 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="432" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Lost after an upgrade</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="116" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="138">Kept as ordinary files</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="165" x2="214" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 160 L224 165 L214 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Ordinary files</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="165" x2="422" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 160 L432 165 L422 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Open them in any app</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A single library also does not fit how I keep my files. Recent work
        is on the main drive of my laptop, and older shoots move to other
        drives. I do not want hundreds of gigabytes in a library that I might
        not be able to open in a few years.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon, I started a basic version. I did not try to copy
        Lightroom. I wanted the smallest thing that could get these photos to
        my friends. This was the list:
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li><strong>Open a folder and show the photos in it.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5,</strong> to go through a shoot quickly and mark the keepers.</li>
        <li><strong>Filter by rating,</strong> to edit only the photos that I like.</li>
        <li><strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows, and the other basic settings.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Keep all adjustments non-destructive.</strong> The editor never changes the original file, so I can always undo an edit.</li>
        <li><strong>Export selected photos to JPEG,</strong> ready to send.</li>
        <li><strong>Run on a Mac.</strong></li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What else I am trying to learn</h2>
      <p>
        Coding agents write most of the code. An
        agent gives you code that compiles and runs, but that code can give
        the wrong result some of the time, or crash in a case that nobody
        tested. A photo editor
        shows these problems quickly. If a slider lags, or a folder of RAW
        files uses all your memory, you notice immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        So LightPhotos is two projects for me. It is the photo editor that I
        wanted. It is also how I learn what it takes to build software with
        agents and still care about quality and speed. Most of these posts are
        about that.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        In the next post, I go through the big design decisions in LightPhotos
        and what I gave up for each one. If this sounds familiar, I would like
        to hear how you dealt with it.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/why-rust.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Why Rust</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    const fig = document.getElementById("needs-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const names = ["Lightroom 6", "Apple Photos", "Luminar AI", "Darktable and friends", "Lightroom subscription", "LightPhotos"];
      const buttons = [...fig.querySelectorAll("[data-need]")];
      const rows = [...fig.querySelectorAll("[data-row]")];
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");

      function update() {
        const on = buttons.map((b) => b.getAttribute("aria-pressed") === "true");
        fig.querySelectorAll("svg [data-col]").forEach((el) => {
          el.setAttribute("opacity", on[+el.dataset.col] ? "1" : "0.25");
        });
        const fits = [];
        rows.forEach((row, i) => {
          const fails = +row.dataset.fails;
          const fit = fails < 0 || !on[fails];
          row.setAttribute("opacity", fit ? "1" : "0.55");
          row.querySelector("rect").setAttribute("opacity", fit ? "1" : "0");
          if (fit) fits.push(names[i]);
        });
        const count = on.filter(Boolean).length;
        readout.textContent = `${count} of 5 needs on. Options that fit: ${fits.join(", ")}.`;
      }

      buttons.forEach((b) =>
        b.addEventListener("click", () => {
          b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", b.getAttribute("aria-pressed") === "true" ? "false" : "true");
          update();
        })
      );
    }
  </script>
</Base>
